Title: FATak
Credit: A Three-Act Play
Author: Julian

FADE IN:

ACT I

EXT. FATak'S STREET - AFTERNOON

A narrow, sun-washed street. Scooters lean against compound walls. A pressure cooker whistles somewhere. The smell of fried onions, cardamom, cloves and chicken drifts through the air.

FATAK, 17, lively, restless, and perpetually hungry, sits on a doorstep with a cup of tea.

He takes a sip.

FATAK
My tea is getting cold.

He looks toward the house.

FATAK
Amma!

No answer.

FATAK
Amma!

AMMA, 45, appears at the doorway carrying a kitchen towel.

AMMA
What now?

FATAK
What are we having for lunch?

AMMA
Food.

FATAK
What food?

AMMA
The kind that disappears when you put it in your mouth.

FATAK
Amma.

AMMA
Biryani.

Fatak sits upright as though someone has switched on a lamp inside him.

FATAK
Chicken?

AMMA
Chicken.

FATAK
With potatoes?

AMMA
With potatoes.

FATAK
The big potatoes?

AMMA
There are no small potatoes in this house. You eat all the small ones first.

Fatak grins.

FATAK
I knew today would be a good day.

AMMA
Every day is a good day when you are not the one cooking.

Fatak takes another long sip of tea.

FATAK
I have been thinking.

AMMA
Dangerous.

FATAK
We should have biryani every day.

AMMA
That is not thinking. That is a stomach speaking through you.

FATAK
Imagine it. Every day. Lunch. Biryani.

AMMA
And dinner?

FATAK
Biryani.

AMMA
Breakfast?

FATAK
Tea.

AMMA
Only tea?

FATAK
Biryani would be strange at breakfast.

AMMA
Good. There is hope for civilization.

She disappears into the kitchen.

Fatak smiles into his tea.

CUT TO:

INT. FATak'S HOUSE - LUNCHTIME

A steel plate of chicken biryani.

Fatak eats with full concentration.

He separates one potato. Smells it. Eats it.

He closes his eyes.

FATAK
Perfect.

His younger sister, LILI, 10, watches him.

LILI
You say that every day.

FATAK
Because every day it is true.

LILI
Doesn't it get boring?

FATAK
Does the moon get boring because it comes back every month?

LILI
The moon is not chicken.

FATAK
You are very young.

Lili steals a piece of chicken.

FATAK
Hey!

LILI
The moon shared.

Amma enters.

AMMA
Let her eat.

FATAK
She has her own food.

AMMA
And yet she is your sister.

Fatak reluctantly pushes another piece toward Lili.

LILI
See? He can share.

FATAK
One piece is sharing. Two pieces is charity. Three pieces is robbery.

They laugh.

A MONTAGE OF DAYS:

Fatak eats biryani.

Fatak drinks tea.

Fatak asks Amma about biryani.

Fatak carries a plate to the courtyard.

Fatak studies with the smell of biryani in the room.

Fatak works.

Biryani.

Tea.

Biryani.

The routine becomes almost ceremonial.

EXT. STREET - EVENING

Fatak and three friends play cricket with a battered tennis ball.

The friends are RIZWAN, 17, dramatic; MALA, 16, observant; and KIRAN, 18, always hungry but always joking.

RIZWAN
Run!

KIRAN
I am running!

MALA
You are walking!

KIRAN
I am preserving energy.

Rizwan hits the ball.

THWACK.

The ball flies over the neighboring wall.

Everyone groans.

RIZWAN
Fatak, your turn.

FATAK
Why me?

MALA
Because you are the one who said, "I can climb that wall."

FATAK
I said I could climb a wall. I did not say I wanted to.

KIRAN
Go, biryani prince.

FATAK
Do not call me that.

KIRAN
Why? It is accurate.

Fatak climbs the wall.

He drops into the neighboring compound.

EXT. NEIGHBORING COMPOUND - CONTINUOUS

A quiet corner.

An OLD MAN, KAREEM, 65, sits on the ground beside a dented metal bowl.

In it: a little plain rice. No meat. No gravy. No spice.

He eats slowly.

Fatak freezes.

The man looks up.

KAREEM
Looking for something?

FATAK
The ball.

Kareem points.

KAREEM
Behind you.

Fatak picks it up.

He cannot stop looking at the bowl.

KAREEM
It is only rice.

FATAK
I know.

KAREEM
Rice is also food.

FATAK
I know.

KAREEM
You look surprised.

FATAK
I suppose I am.

KAREEM
By the rice?

FATAK
By how little there is.

Kareem considers him.

KAREEM
You can have too much of a good thing without noticing the little that is enough.

Fatak doesn't know what to say.

KAREEM
Your friends are waiting.

Fatak nods.

FATAK
Yes.

He leaves.

EXT. STREET - MOMENTS LATER

Rizwan grabs the ball.

RIZWAN
What took you so long?

FATAK
Nothing.

MALA
You look strange.

FATAK
Do I?

KIRAN
Maybe you have finally run out of appetite.

Fatak watches the neighboring compound.

FATAK
Let's play.

They resume the game.

But Fatak is no longer playing.

His mind is elsewhere.

INT. FATak'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Dinner.

Fatak barely touches his food.

AMMA
Are you ill?

FATAK
No.

LILI
Did someone die?

AMMA
Lili.

LILI
He looks like somebody died.

FATAK
Nobody died.

AMMA
Then eat.

Fatak eats one mouthful.

AMMA
Only one?

FATAK
Amma...

AMMA
Hmm?

FATAK
Do you think everyone eats like us?

Amma sits.

AMMA
No.

FATAK
Why?

AMMA
Because people are different.

FATAK
No. I mean... why should some people have more than enough and others have almost nothing?

Amma takes a breath.

AMMA
That is a question bigger than your dinner plate.

FATAK
Maybe not.

Lili looks at him.

FATAK
Maybe it starts with the plate.

Silence.

ACT I END


ACT II

INT. FATak'S HOUSE - NEXT DAY - AFTERNOON

The familiar ritual.

Biryani is served.

Fatak stares at it.

Amma waits.

AMMA
What is wrong with it?

FATAK
Nothing.

AMMA
Too much salt?

FATAK
No.

AMMA
Too little chicken?

FATAK
No.

AMMA
Then eat.

Fatak carefully divides the biryani into two portions.

Amma watches.

AMMA
What are you doing?

FATAK
Changing something.

AMMA
What?

FATAK
My share.

He eats half.

AMMA
And the other half?

FATAK
Tomorrow.

AMMA
It will not be biryani tomorrow.

FATAK
Then it will become tomorrow's problem.

AMMA
You cannot save tomorrow by starving today.

FATAK
I am not starving.

AMMA
You are sixteen steps away from a dramatic song about famine.

FATAK
I just want to eat less.

AMMA
Why?

FATAK
Because somewhere somebody is eating less because they have to.

Amma softens.

AMMA
And you think eating less will feed them?

FATAK
I don't know.

AMMA
Then what are you trying to do?

FATAK
Not pretend I don't know.

Amma sits beside him.

AMMA
That is a beginning.

EXT. TEA STALL - EVENING

Fatak sits with Rizwan, Mala, and Kiran.

A kettle whistles.

TEA SELLER
Four teas?

FATAK
Three.

Everyone looks at him.

KIRAN
Three?

FATAK
I am cutting down.

RIZWAN
On tea?

FATAK
A little.

KIRAN
This is serious.

MALA
What happened?

Fatak tells them about Kareem.

The jokes fade.

RIZWAN
So what will you do?

FATAK
Eat less.

KIRAN
That's it?

FATAK
For now.

MALA
That sounds like a private promise, not a solution.

FATAK
Maybe private promises are where solutions begin.

KIRAN
Or maybe you just want to lose weight without admitting it.

Fatak smiles.

FATAK
I have seen you eat four samosas.

KIRAN
Research.

MALA
Research into what?

KIRAN
The structural integrity of the human stomach.

They laugh.

Then Mala gets serious.

MALA
If you really want to help, learn where the food goes.

FATAK
What do you mean?

MALA
Restaurants throw away food. Shops throw away food. Weddings throw away food. Houses throw away food.

FATAK
So what do we do?

MALA
Find out.

MONTAGE:

Fatak and his friends visit a small restaurant after closing.

They see unsold food being discarded.

A shopkeeper throws bruised vegetables into a bin.

Fatak carries leftover bread to Kareem.

Kareem refuses.

KAREEM
Do not turn me into a lesson.

FATAK
I am not.

KAREEM
Then don't bring me food to make yourself feel better.

Fatak is stunned.

KAREEM
Give it because you want another person to eat. Not because you want to become a good person.

FATAK
How do I know the difference?

KAREEM
You don't. That is why you keep checking yourself.

Fatak sits beside him.

They share silence.

INT. FATak'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Fatak catches Amma measuring rice.

FATAK
How much rice do we usually cook?

AMMA
Enough for everyone.

FATAK
That is not a number.

AMMA
It is the best number.

FATAK
Suppose we cooked less.

AMMA
Then suppose everyone remained hungry.

FATAK
What about leftovers?

AMMA
You have finally noticed them.

Fatak looks embarrassed.

AMMA
You think the promise is only about eating less.

FATAK
Isn't it?

AMMA
No. It is about wasting less of what you already have.

FATAK
So I should finish everything.

AMMA
Not everything. Only what you take.

A beat.

AMMA
The world does not become fair because you leave food on a plate.

FATAK
Then what?

AMMA
The world becomes a little less careless when you pay attention.

EXT. COMMUNITY LANE - DAY

Fatak and his friends put up a hand-painted sign:

TAKE WHAT YOU NEED. LEAVE WHAT YOU DON'T.

A skeptical NEIGHBOR, MR. SEN, 55, watches.

MR. SEN
What is this?

FATAK
A food shelf.

MR. SEN
Who gave you permission?

FATAK
No one.

MR. SEN
Then who takes responsibility?

FATAK
We do.

MR. SEN
You are children.

MALA
That is temporary.

Mr. Sen studies them.

MR. SEN
People will misuse it.

FATAK
Maybe.

MR. SEN
People will take more than they need.

FATAK
Maybe.

MR. SEN
People will laugh.

FATAK
Probably.

MR. SEN
Then why do it?

FATAK
Because there are already people with too little.

Mr. Sen has no immediate answer.

He puts a bag of rice on the shelf.

MR. SEN
For the record, I am doing this because my wife cooks too much.

MALA
Of course.

He walks away.

The shelf slowly fills.

Then it empties.

Again.

And again.

ACT II END


ACT III

EXT. COMMUNITY LANE - MONTHS LATER - MORNING

The food shelf has become a modest community pantry.

A hand-painted board reads:

FOOD FOR TODAY.

People come and go.

Fatak carries a sack of rice.

Kiran counts tins.

Rizwan repairs a shelf.

Mala keeps a notebook.

MALA
We served seventy-three portions this week.

KIRAN
Seventy-four.

MALA
You ate one.

KIRAN
I was testing quality.

FATAK
You are very committed to public service.

Kiran bows.

KAREEM enters.

He carries something wrapped in cloth.

KAREEM
For you.

Fatak opens it.

Inside is a small brass bowl.

FATAK
What's this?

KAREEM
My mother used it.

FATAK
I can't take it.

KAREEM
You can.

FATAK
Why?

KAREEM
Because a bowl should not sit empty forever.

Fatak is moved.

KAREEM
And because you once looked at my rice as though it had broken your heart.

FATAK
It did.

KAREEM
Good.

FATAK
Good?

KAREEM
A heart that never breaks is not much use to anybody.

Amma arrives with a large pot.

AMMA
Move. Move. Move. Hot biryani.

Fatak's eyes widen.

KIRAN
The prince has returned.

FATAK
Don't call me that.

AMMA
I made enough for everyone.

FATAK
How much did you make?

AMMA
Enough.

FATAK
That's not a number.

AMMA
It is when you have been cooking for forty-five years.

Laughter.

They serve biryani to the people gathered.

Fatak takes a portion.

It is modest.

He sits beside Kareem.

KAREEM
You used to eat twice this much.

FATAK
I know.

KAREEM
Do you miss it?

Fatak looks at the plate.

FATAK
Sometimes.

KAREEM
Then eat it.

FATAK
What?

KAREEM
You made a promise not to eat more than you need. You did not promise to stop being hungry.

Fatak smiles.

He takes a bite.

KAREEM
Good?

FATAK
Perfect.

KAREEM
Doesn't it get boring?

Fatak looks at him.

FATAK
Never.

Across the lane, a CHILD watches the food.

Fatak notices.

He gestures.

FATAK
Come.

The child approaches.

Fatak moves his plate over.

The child takes a piece.

Fatak laughs.

FATAK
Take another.

The child does.

Fatak watches, not possessive now, but delighted.

MALA
Fatak.

FATAK
Hmm?

MALA
What do you think your promise actually changed?

Fatak thinks.

FATAK
Me.

MALA
Only you?

Fatak looks around.

At the shelf.

At Amma.

At Kareem.

At the friends.

At the people eating.

FATAK
I don't know.

KAREEM
That is the right answer.

RIZWAN
But something changed.

KIRAN
Definitely my diet.

They laugh.

Fatak stands.

He walks toward the food shelf.

There is one empty basket.

He puts the brass bowl inside it.

FATAK
For whoever comes tomorrow.

Amma watches him.

AMMA
And what will you eat tomorrow?

Fatak smiles.

FATAK
Whatever is enough.

A breeze moves through the lane.

The sign above the shelf trembles:

FOOD FOR TODAY.

Fatak looks at it.

Then at the people.

Then at his half-eaten biryani.

He takes another bite.

BLACKOUT.

END.
